With the rapid technological advancement in [[Færrin]] these past few centuries, one can almost forget the underlying magic backbone of the continent. Electricity may be commonplace now, but just 40 years ago, if one needed to see at night, they burned whale oil or summoned a light spirit. Long-distance communication happened by familiar, not by radio signal, and a "refrigerator" was a box with a thermal stabilizer enchantment.

As these new advancements made creature comforts cheaper, much of the old guard found itself obsolete. No one would pay an arm and a leg for a pair of sending stones when you could buy walkie-talkies for pennies on the dollar in your local general store (not to mention phones, which weren't bound to a single receiver!)

This rapid change in the status quo may have toppled many former giants, but some [[Org/index|Orgs]] knew how to capitalize on the volatility. Enter Concordia. Some breakthroughs in what would be called the "assembly line" and a hard pivot from scrolls to dishwashers, freezers, and air conditioners meant that when the wave of the future hit [[Geography/Calaria/index|Calaria]], it had Concordia's name on it.

Their secret? Just use their magic prowess earlier in the production chain! Concordia employs skilled mages of all creeds to design and build their assembly lines. Steel that twists itself into shape! Self-assembling motors! And why stop at household appliances when they already have the workforce? They make watches, televisions, and construction machinery, too! If you can buy something, Concordia probably makes it.

Where [[Concordia]] distinguishes itself is in the shop floor — a consumer-goods giant in the mould of the great home-product conglomerates, it turns its hand to appliances and domestic wares, and polices its branding with unusual rigour; any Fane it maintains across multiple [[Phenomena/Hearts|Hearts]] is kept to a uniform aesthetic, so that a Concordia storefront in one district is near-indistinguishable from one found streets away in another.

Crawford Reeves and Louis Maller are of Concordia, and are two of the three parties hunting keystone 14 in [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]].

Concordia's yellow raiment carries a smiley face on the chest, and its uniformed hands are called Happy Time Enforcers.

When the Party came to the [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Sableclutch/Prime Meridian Sub Transit Nexus|Sub Transit Nexus]], two Concordia agents, [[Org/Concordia/People/Crawford Reeves|Crawford Reeves]] and [[Org/Concordia/People/Louis Maller|Louis Maller]], arrived after them with six Happy Time Enforcers and took the gatehouse outside. Reeves read from a clipboard out of Concordia's written charter, that the Party were happiness sinks and had five seconds to hand over everything, since at least one of them held something needed for the happiness of all. They fought the Party and Prime Meridian's staff and lost. Reeves' hair cut [[People/Benny|Benny]] and a gear the Heart had burned into his brow broke, and he came apart in rust. [[People/Johnny|Johnny]]'s spell left Maller clapping, and Benny ran him through. The last enforcers fell as a blue light flashed from their eyes.

The enforcers sent to recover [[Phenomena/Keystone|keystone 14]] flashed a blue light from their eyes as they died. [[People/Anzu|Anzu]], who had seen the constructs of [[Org/Belvedere|Belvedere]] give off the same glow along their skin and veins, took it for some mechanical augmentation, though he had not examined them. [[Org/Concordia/People/Crawford Reeves|Crawford Reeves]] carried a small inert green stone, and [[Org/Concordia/People/Louis Maller|Louis Maller]] wore boots with detachable clockwork heels.
